entioned in SRC_002, proposing that expertise is the ability to weight variables rather than just identify them; this is refuted if a 'weighted checklist' (assigning numerical values to items) allows non-experts to achieve expert-level reliability without domain knowledge.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"d36c185071e00b2be48ab692c428ef9c19852ca47861933528f84363962dcf9f"},{"role":"dependence","target":"5d003bb87b08cf8a385a535ef9316fb8941d8abbafdcf3dedf14bb18a2cc8357"}],"typicality":0.4},{"content":"The non-expert panel's reliability inherits the fragility of the checklist's design as described in SRC_004; specifically, the panel's success is a function of the checklist's completeness, and this is refuted if the introduction of a 'trap item' (a false prompt that feels true) causes the panel to prioritize compliance over objective verification.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"099a3bb5ce1903af7536de18a8c4c6a58bb2fef89caec4e8e0119db7fa6b9cf8"}],"typicality":0.6},{"content":"The 'Systemic Blind Spot' mechanism in SRC_004 reduces to a shared knowledge error across the panel; this is refuted if the non-experts are selected from sufficiently diverse cognitive backgrounds such that their 'blind spots' are uncorrelated, thereby restoring the additive reliability described in SRC_001.","evidence_refs":[],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"099a3bb5ce1903af7536de18a8c4c6a58bb2fef89caec4e8e0119db7fa6b9cf8"},{"role":"dependence","target":"0a07b5048a67c4213e4e113cc978ea24c78a2e1e847b7b43e5b18a021b1dc074"}],"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}